script:
export const meta = {
  name: "sbps-radar-improvements",
  description: "Audit SBPS radar codebase and research external data feeds; synthesize prioritized improvement opportunities into a markdown report",
  phases: [{ title: "Investigate" }, { title: "Synthesize" }],
};

const itemSchema = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    items: {
      type: "array",
      items: {
        type: "object",
        properties: {
          title: { type: "string" },
          kind: { type: "string", enum: ["internal", "feed"] },
          description: { type: "string" },
          alphaRationale: { type: "string" },
          feasibility: { type: "string" },
          effort: { type: "string", enum: ["S", "M", "L"] },
          phase: { type: "string", enum: ["1", "2", "3"] },
          priority: { type: "number" },
          evidence: { type: "string" },
          source: { type: "string" },
        },
        required: ["title", "kind", "description", "alphaRationale", "feasibility", "effort", "phase", "priority"],
      },
    },
  },
  required: ["items"],
};

const bizCtx = `PROJECT: SBPS Supply Radar — AI signal monitor for Single Barrel Power Solutions, a turnkey broker in industrial power generation equipment.
CORE THESIS: Equipment availability IS the product (supply-starved market). The alpha is TIME-TO-SIGNAL — finding surplus/available equipment before broker chains form and mark up prices. SBPS is sole intermediary: title holder <-> SBPS <-> buyer, transparent 1/1/1 commission.
MODEL: title holder <-> SBPS <-> buyer. Global scope (50/60Hz, voltage, grid code, logistics are hard match gates). Title-holder verification is non-negotiable (distinguish real owners from brokers claiming to represent sellers).
PIPELINE: Collect -> Classify (LLM) -> Enrich -> Cross-reference contacts -> Score -> Call sheet. SQLite store with dedup.
CURRENT SOURCES: EIA-860 (plant retirements/generator status), SEC EDGAR 8-K (mergers/bankruptcies/asset dispositions), FERC eLibrary (project withdrawals/permit cancellations), PACER (bankruptcy filings with listed equipment assets).
KNOWN STATUS (from handoff): EIA collector yields 3892 raw signals -> 521 after dedup -> 39 unique companies. SEC 8-K via Atom feed works. Buyer matching: 114 buyers loaded, 514/521 signals match >=1 buyer. Contact matching: 39 seller companies ALL blind spots (none in buyer list). Call sheet ranked by alpha. SQLite dedup + actionable_contacts.csv export. 32 tests passing, lint clean.
PHASE PLAN: Phase 1 (now) = public signal monitoring + call sheet. Phase 2 = cert partner intake, title verification, frequency/voltage matching, blind-spot discovery, persistence hardening. Phase 3 = auction house foundation, insurance partnerships, broker registry.
NAMED HIGH-ALPHA FUTURE SOURCES: certification partners (inspect equipment before any sale -> see distress FIRST; highest alpha but needs relationship setup), auction houses, insurance partners, broker registry. Also mentioned: behind-the-meter/data-center self-gen, turbine blade failures -> repair quotes -> sell decision, conference attendee lists as discovery, new surplus sitting in warehouses (cancelled projects, bankrupt developers).`;

phase("Investigate");
const [audit, feeds] = await parallel([
  () => agent(
    `You are auditing the SBPS Supply Radar Python codebase to find concrete IMPROVEMENT opportunities.\n\n${bizCtx}\n\nYOUR TASK: Read the actual source under src/Sbps/ (files: cli.py, models.py, store.py, config.py, classifier.py, enricher.py, matcher.py, scorer.py, verifier.py, call_sheet.py, buyer_matcher.py, cert_intake.py, llm/client.py, collectors/base.py, collectors/eia.py, collectors/sec.py, collectors/ferc.py, collectors/pacer.py). Read the ones relevant to each finding.\n\nIdentify concrete improvement opportunities. Look for: collectors specced but not fully built (FERC, PACER), weak/naive scoring, missing or shallow enrichment, LLM classifier quality/prompt issues, missing error handling/retries/rate-limiting, pagination/dedup gaps, no scheduling/automation, title verification not implemented, frequency/voltage matching gaps, blind-spot discovery logic quality, buyer-matcher match-gate rigor, observability/logging, test coverage holes, config/secrets handling, data freshness. Also flag quick wins vs deeper work.\n\nFor EACH opportunity return one item. kind="internal". evidence MUST cite real file paths/functions you actually read. alphaRationale must tie to time-to-signal or deal-quality. feasibility = what it takes. effort S/M/L. phase = which phase it maps to. priority 1 (highest) to 5. Aim for 8-14 grounded items.\n\nReturn JSON matching the schema.`,
    { label: "codebase-audit", tier: "medium", schema: itemSchema },
  ),
  () => agent(
    `You are researching EXTERNAL data feeds / signal sources that could enhance the SBPS Supply Radar's time-to-signal alpha beyond the 4 current sources (EIA-860, SEC 8-K, FERC, PACER).\n\n${bizCtx}\n\nYOUR TASK: Research and evaluate candidate external signal sources for surplus/available industrial power equipment (gas/steam turbines, generators, transformers, switchgear, etc.). Use PRIMARY sources (official sites, APIs, docs) and cite URLs. Cover:\n- The NAMED Phase 2/3 sources: certification/inspection partners (e.g. who inspects this equipment, any registries?), auction houses for industrial/power equipment, insurance partners (recovery/retirement signals), broker registries.\n- NEWLY discovered high-alpha sources, e.g.: industry-specific auction/marketplace platforms, OEM remarketing/used-equipment portals, decommissioning/project-cancellation databases, data-center capacity market signals, grid/operator retirement announcements (ISO/RTO interconnection queues, generator deactivation notices), EPA/environmental retirement & compliance signals, shipping/port/customs import-export signals, insurance/loss registers, conference/exhibition attendee/exhibitor lists, trade-press distress feeds, LinkedIn/company signals, international equivalents (50Hz markets — Europe/Asia/Africa grid operators, ENTSO-E, etc.).\n\nFor EACH source return one item. kind="feed". evidence/source MUST include a real URL. alphaRationale must tie to time-to-signal (earliest sight of distress/availability). feasibility = free vs paid + access method (API/scrape/relationship/manual). effort S/M/L. phase 1/2/3. priority 1-5. Prioritize sources that see availability BEFORE broker chains form. Aim for 10-16 grounded items.\n\nReturn JSON matching the schema.`,
    { label: "data-feed-research", tier: "medium", schema: itemSchema },
  ),
]);

const auditItems = (audit && audit.items) || [];
const feedItems = (feeds && feeds.items) || [];
log(`audit items: ${auditItems.length}, feed items: ${feedItems.length}`);

phase("Synthesize");
const combined = auditItems.concat(feedItems);
const doc = await agent(
  `You are writing the final deliverable for the SBPS client: a prioritized improvement-opportunities report for their supply radar. Below is a JSON array of candidate items (internal codebase improvements kind="internal", and external data feeds kind="feed"), each with title, description, alphaRationale, feasibility, effort, phase, priority, evidence, source.\n\n${JSON.stringify(combined)}\n\nWrite ONE complete Markdown document. Requirements:\n- Title: "SBPS Supply Radar — Improvement Opportunities".\n- Short intro (3-4 sentences) framing the goal: raise time-to-signal alpha and deal quality.\n- "Prioritization method" line: alpha (time-to-signal / deal-quality impact) x feasibility (access + effort).\n- Section A "Internal Pipeline Improvements": a markdown table with columns Title | Alpha | Feasibility | Effort | Phase | Evidence(file). Then a "Top internal picks" bullet list (3-5) with one-line rationale each.\n- Section B "External Data Feeds": a markdown table with columns Source | Signal | Alpha | Feasibility(free/paid + access) | Effort | Phase | URL. Then a "Top feed picks" bullet list (3-5) with one-line rationale.\n- Section "Top 5 — do next": a ranked combined list across both sections, each with a 1-2 sentence why-now.\n- Section "Phase roadmap": 3-4 bullets mapping work to Phase 1/2/3.\n- Be concrete and specific; cite the evidence/URLs from the items. No generic filler, no hype. If an item is weak, drop it rather than pad.\n- Output the Markdown document ONLY, no wrapping commentary.`,
  { label: "synthesize-report", tier: "big" },
);

return {
  internalCount: auditItems.length,
  feedCount: feedItems.length,
  auditFailed: audit === null,
  feedsFailed: feeds === null,
  doc,
};
